WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.500
Welcome back to chapter one: algebraic proofs. In this video, we meet identities, and learn how to prove that two expressions are equal.

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:21.067
There are four lessons to cover. First, what an identity is. Then, three kinds of proof: simple equalities, equalities that come with a condition, and equalities built on ratios.

3
00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:27.167
Picture a heart monitor in a hospital. Every beat of the heart draws a wave across the screen.

4
00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:37.900
Behind that wave sits a huge, complicated formula. If a tiny pacemaker had to work it out in full, every millisecond, its battery would drain dangerously fast.

5
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:48.400
So engineers swap the bulky formula for a much simpler one. But that swap is only safe if the simple formula gives exactly the same output, every single time.

6
00:00:48.800 --> 00:01:00.167
A guarantee like that is called an identity. Two expressions that look completely different, yet always produce the same value. Today, we learn how to recognise one, and how to prove it.

7
00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:08.667
First, we need to separate two ideas that look alike. Here is an equation: two X plus one, equals seven.

8
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:16.367
Draw each side as a graph. The two lines cross at just one point, where X equals three. For any other value of X, the two sides disagree.

9
00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:22.700
Now look at this one. X plus one, all squared, equals X squared, plus two X, plus one.

10
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:34.167
Draw both sides, and something different happens. The two curves lie exactly on top of each other. Slide X anywhere you like, and the left-hand side and the right-hand side always match.

11
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:43.633
An equality that holds true for any value of X is called an identity in X. An equation asks, which X works? An identity says, every X works.

12
00:01:44.833 --> 00:01:50.133
Identities give us a powerful tool. Suppose two quadratics are equal for every value of X.

13
00:01:50.533 --> 00:01:57.733
Change even one coefficient, and the two curves pull apart. So the two sides must be the same expression, term by term.

14
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:05.333
The X squared coefficients match. The X coefficients match. And the constants match. This is called comparing coefficients.

15
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:21.200
For example, if A X squared, plus B X, plus C, is identical to three X squared, minus five X, plus two, then A must be three, B must be negative five, and C must be two.

16
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:28.800
The recipe is simple. Arrange both sides in descending powers of X. Then compare the coefficients, one power at a time.

17
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.533
Let's try it. Find the constants A, B and C that make this an identity in X.

18
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:42.500
The right-hand side is not in order yet. So we expand the brackets, and collect the terms in descending powers of X.

19
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:51.867
That gives A times X squared. Then two A plus B, times X. And finally a constant: A plus B plus C.

20
00:02:52.267 --> 00:03:01.000
Now compare with the left-hand side. Careful: there is no X term on the left, so its coefficient is zero. That gives us three small equations.

21
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:12.200
A equals one. Then two plus B is zero, so B is negative two. And one, minus two, plus C is six, so C equals seven.

22
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:23.900
Let's test our answer with one value. Put X equals zero. The left-hand side is six. The right-hand side is one, minus two, plus seven, which is also six. It works.

23
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:32.700
Identities can also involve fractions. Here, one fraction is split into two simpler ones, and we need to find A and B.

24
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:40.233
The textbook's advice is to clear the denominators first. So multiply both sides by X plus two, times X minus three.

25
00:03:40.633 --> 00:03:48.900
On the left, the whole denominator cancels, leaving three X plus one. On the right, each constant is left with the bracket its fraction was missing.

26
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:57.400
Expand, and arrange in descending powers. The coefficient of X is A plus B, and the constant is negative three A, plus two B.

27
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:08.200
Now compare coefficients. A plus B equals three, and negative three A plus two B equals one. Solving this pair gives A equals one, and B equals two.

28
00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:19.567
Now for proofs. To prove that a left-hand side equals a right-hand side, the textbook gives one clear rule. Simplify, until both sides become the same expression.

29
00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:29.833
Often, simplifying just one side is enough. We claim that X plus Y, all squared, minus X minus Y, all squared, equals four X Y.

30
00:04:30.233 --> 00:04:33.533
Start with the left-hand side, and expand both squares.

31
00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:46.833
Remove the brackets. The minus sign flips every sign in the second bracket. Now the X squared terms cancel, and so do the Y squared terms. What remains is four X Y, exactly the right-hand side.

32
00:04:47.233 --> 00:04:58.933
You can even see it in a picture. Take a big square with side X plus Y, and remove a small square with side X minus Y from its middle. What is left is four rectangles, each X by Y.

33
00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:06.300
Sometimes neither side is simple. Then we work on both sides, separately. Here is a famous example.

34
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:11.033
Expand the left-hand side. Four products appear. Call this result one.

35
00:05:11.433 --> 00:05:22.800
Now, on its own, expand the right-hand side. The two squared brackets give six terms. But the middle terms, minus two A B C D, and plus two A B C D, cancel each other.

36
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:32.267
Rearranged, the right-hand side has the very same four terms. Call this result two. Both sides have arrived at the same expression, so the equality is proven.

37
00:05:32.667 --> 00:05:44.267
Notice what we did not do. We never moved terms across the equals sign, and we never multiplied both sides by anything. Those moves assume the equality is already true, and that is the very thing we are trying to prove.

38
00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:52.767
Some equalities are only true under a condition. For example: if A plus B plus C equals zero, prove this equality.

39
00:05:53.167 --> 00:06:05.733
Try it with numbers first. One, two, and negative three add up to zero. Their squares add up to fourteen. And the right-hand side also comes to fourteen. Now we prove it always works.

40
00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:14.533
The trick is to use the condition to get rid of one letter. From A plus B plus C equals zero, we can write C as negative A, minus B.

41
00:06:14.933 --> 00:06:23.267
Substitute that into the left-hand side. Expand the square, collect like terms, and we get two A squared, plus two A B, plus two B squared.

42
00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:30.333
Now substitute into the right-hand side. Replace every C, multiply out, and collect like terms inside the bracket.

43
00:06:30.733 --> 00:06:42.733
Finally, multiply by negative two. Two A squared, plus two A B, plus two B squared. The same expression again. With C eliminated, both sides agree, and the proof is complete.

44
00:06:43.933 --> 00:06:57.067
Our last tool is for proportions. A proportion says that two ratios are equal, like A over B equals C over D. For instance, six over three, and ten over five, are both equal to two.

45
00:06:57.467 --> 00:07:05.000
The trick is to give that shared ratio a name. Call it K. Then A equals B times K, and C equals D times K.

46
00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.333
Let's prove this equality. On the left-hand side, replace A with B K.

47
00:07:09.733 --> 00:07:17.667
B squared is a common factor, on the top and on the bottom, so it cancels. We are left with K, over K squared plus one.

48
00:07:18.067 --> 00:07:32.000
Do the same on the right-hand side, replacing C with D K. This time D squared cancels, and again we are left with K, over K squared plus one. Both sides match, so the equality holds.

49
00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:40.667
The same idea handles a three-part ratio. Suppose X to Y to Z is two to three to five, and we want the value of this fraction.

50
00:07:41.067 --> 00:07:48.133
Let K be a constant, and write X equals two K, Y equals three K, and Z equals five K.

51
00:07:48.533 --> 00:08:02.300
Substitute into the top. Twelve K squared, plus fifteen K squared, plus thirty K squared, makes fifty-seven K squared. The bottom gives four, plus nine, plus twenty-five. That is thirty-eight K squared.

52
00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:10.867
The K squared cancels, and fifty-seven over thirty-eight simplifies to three over two. Whatever the value of K, the answer never changes.

53
00:08:11.267 --> 00:08:21.833
Test it. When K is one, the three numbers are two, three and five. When K is ten, they are twenty, thirty and fifty. Either way, the fraction comes out as three over two.

54
00:08:23.033 --> 00:08:30.233
Let's recap. An identity is true for every value of X, and we find its unknown constants by comparing coefficients.

55
00:08:30.633 --> 00:08:37.700
To prove an equality, simplify the left-hand side, the right-hand side, or both, until they become the same expression.

56
00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:44.667
If there is a condition, use it to eliminate a variable. And if there is a proportion, set the ratio equal to K.

57
00:08:45.067 --> 00:08:55.400
Now it is your turn. Pause the video, and try this problem from the textbook. The ratio of A to B to C is two to three to four. Find the value of the fraction on the screen.

58
00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:02.600
Did you get twenty-six over twenty-nine? Well done. In the next video, we move from equalities to inequalities.

